Build the complete local-first application shell, orchestration layer, browser management system, session handling, traffic capture pipeline, user interface, and supporting infrastructure that hosts and drives the four core detection engines (Authz Matrix Runner, Payment & Webhook Integrity Harness, Race/Concurrency Engine, and Parameter & Business-Logic Fuzzer). The application must run entirely on the user’s Windows machine. The only action required from the user is to log in once (or a few times for multi-account testing) inside a controlled browser; after that confirmation, everything else is fully automated.

### High-level architecture
- A local web application (preferred stack: Electron + React/Next.js or a clean Node.js + modern frontend) that the user launches on Windows.
- When a scan is started, the application launches one or more fully isolated, controlled Chromium instances via Playwright (preferred) or an equivalent reliable browser automation library.
- The controlled browser is the single source of truth for authentication state, cookies, localStorage, sessionStorage, IndexedDB, service workers, and all subsequent network traffic.
- All traffic originating from the controlled browser is transparently captured, decoded, categorized, and fed to the four engines.
- The application keeps the controlled browser(s) alive and logged-in for the entire duration of the scan, automatically handling token refresh, cookie updates, and session continuity whenever possible.
- Clean separation between the UI/orchestrator layer and the four detection engines so the engines can be updated independently.

### Browser & session management (critical – must be rock-solid)
- “Start Authenticated Browser” button: launches a fresh controlled Chromium window (or headless with optional visible mode), navigates to the user-supplied target URL, and waits.
- User performs login manually in that window (including any MFA, CAPTCHA, or SSO flow). When finished, the user clicks “I am logged in / Session ready” in the local app.
- At that moment the application takes a complete, serializable snapshot of the browser context: all cookies (including HttpOnly), localStorage, sessionStorage, IndexedDB (where accessible), service worker registrations, and current Authorization / CSRF headers.
- The same browser context remains alive and is reused by every engine. If the session expires or a token needs refresh, the application detects it and either refreshes automatically or pauses and notifies the user.
- Multi-account support: the user can repeat the login process for additional accounts (userA, userB, low-priv, different tenant, etc.). Each account becomes a named, persistent context that the Authz Matrix Runner can use simultaneously.
- Ability to open additional tabs or navigate to external domains (payment provider pages, callback URLs, etc.) while still capturing all traffic under the same session.
- Export and import of full browser contexts so sessions can be saved and restored later without re-logging in.
- Graceful handling of common post-login challenges (WAF, bot detection, geo blocks) with clear user feedback.
- Clean shutdown: all controlled browsers are closed, ports released, and state saved when the user stops the scan or closes the application.

### Traffic capture & analysis pipeline
- Full interception of HTTP/HTTPS traffic (and WebSockets if relevant) from the controlled browser, including TLS-terminated requests.
- Real-time decoding of request and response bodies (JSON, form-urlencoded, GraphQL, multipart, etc.).
- Automatic categorization and tagging of endpoints into high-value groups: wallet/balance/credit/debit, order/cart/checkout, payment/confirm/capture, fulfill/redeem/claim, entitlement/download.
- Live traffic history viewer (lightweight Burp-style) with powerful filtering, search, highlighting of monetary parameters, and “send to specific engine” actions.
- Continuous background discovery: as new pages are visited or new API calls appear, endpoints, parameters, and resource IDs are automatically extracted and added to the shared inventory used by all four engines.
- GraphQL-aware parsing (operations, variables, fragments) and REST-aware parameter extraction.

### Orchestration layer
- Central coordinator that receives the live traffic stream and the shared resource inventory and intelligently feeds the four engines.
- Support for sequential, parallel, or selective execution of the engines (user can choose “Run all core engines”, “Authz only”, “Payment + Race”, etc.).
- Goal-directed mode: the user selects or types a high-level intent such as “Increase my balance without payment” or “Obtain items / fulfill orders without paying”. The orchestrator prioritizes the most relevant engines, endpoints, and parameters accordingly.
- Shared session and state store so every engine operates on the exact same authenticated context and can read/write before/after snapshots of balances, order statuses, and entitlements.
- Ability to pause, resume, or stop individual engines without destroying the browser session.
- Progress tracking and live event stream (new endpoints discovered, tests started, findings raised, errors encountered).
- Post-scan aggregation: all findings from the four engines are collected, deduplicated where appropriate, grouped by the two protected outcomes (unauthorized balance credit vs unpaid fulfillment), and prepared for reporting.

### User interface (must be simple, clear, and usable by non-experts)
- Clean modern dashboard showing:
  - Target URL
  - Current login / session status for each account
  - Selected engines and overall progress
  - Live findings counter with severity breakdown
  - One-click “Run Core Scan” and individual engine controls
- Intent selector (dropdown or free-text) that influences prioritization.
- Real-time findings feed: each finding appears as a card with category, short title, severity, and affected outcome (A or B). Clicking opens a detailed evidence view.
- Detailed finding page containing:
  - Full request and response
  - Before/after state diff (balance, order status, entitlement, etc.)
  - Exact parameters that were manipulated
  - Reproduction steps
  - Raw traffic and timestamps
  - One-click copy or export
- Settings panel: concurrency level, request rate limits, which engines to enable, multi-account management, export formats, stealth/aggression options, theme (dark/light).
- Traffic viewer tab with search, filters, and “send to engine” buttons.
- Clear status indicators, progress bars, tooltips, and short plain-language explanations so a non-professional user always understands what is happening and what a finding means.
- Responsive layout, keyboard shortcuts, and non-blocking UI even during high-concurrency races.

### Reporting & evidence
- Automatic generation of a structured JSON report and a human-readable HTML report at the end of a scan.
- Every finding includes a complete, self-contained evidence pack sufficient for reproduction and for submission to a bug-bounty program or internal security team.
- Findings are explicitly mapped to the two outcomes: unauthorized balance/wallet credit (A) or unpaid fulfillment (B).
- Export options: JSON, HTML, Markdown, and optionally a zip containing the evidence files.

### Additional necessary features
- Local encrypted storage of scan configurations, browser contexts, traffic history, and findings (so sensitive session data is protected at rest).
- Robust error handling and recovery: if a page structure changes, a token expires, a request is blocked, or an engine crashes, the application recovers or pauses with a clear message instead of failing silently.
- Rate limiting, polite defaults, and configurable delays so the tool can be used against real targets without immediate IP or account blocking.
- Logging and optional debug mode for troubleshooting.
- Windows-first packaging: simple installer or portable executable, minimal external dependencies, clear first-run experience.
- Modular internal design so the four detection engines remain independently updatable and testable.
- Support for saving and loading full scan profiles (target + accounts + engine settings + intent).

### Quality and usability bar
- After the user clicks “I am logged in”, the rest of the process must be fully automated and require no further manual request crafting.
- The UI must remain responsive and informative even while hundreds of concurrent requests are being fired by the race engine.
- High reliability of session persistence and traffic capture; these are foundational and must not be fragile.
- Clear separation of concerns: browser management, traffic pipeline, orchestration, UI, and the four engines communicate through well-defined interfaces.
- Production-ready code quality, proper error boundaries, and clean shutdown behavior.

Deliver a complete, polished, production-ready local application that seamlessly turns a single (or few) manual login action into deep, automated, high-signal testing of the four critical vulnerability classes. Everything required for controlled browser lifecycle, persistent multi-account sessions, full traffic capture, intelligent orchestration, easy-to-use interface, evidence collection, and reporting must be included and robust. The application exists solely to host and empower the four core engines; its design must make those engines as effective and easy to run as possible for a non-expert user on Windows.
